    "knowledge_point_id": "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660",
    "knowledge_point": "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/8904a5ebafade54a46cee77a2e20cac255c888d257ef1c208b2def32d247f2cd.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{11}{12}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$",
    "worked_solution": "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{11}{12}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{11}{12})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{121}{144}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{121}{144} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{23}{144} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{144}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.$",
